<Page title="Moving from development work into the mineral transition" shortTitle="Career transition guide" kicker="Careers" description="What development professionals bring to mineral-related work, what they would need to add, and how to judge whether a role has real influence."
  standfirst="What carries over from development work, what you would need to learn, and how to tell a role with influence from one without.">
  <p class="notice">Our guidance and interpretation. It does not promise employment.</p>
  <h2>What carries over, and what to add</h2>
  <dl class="facts">
    <dt>Community participation</dt><dd>Carries over to stakeholder engagement and benefit-sharing work. Add: how extractive licensing and land acquisition work, how grievance mechanisms are judged, and the difference between consultation and consent. <a href={url('careers/pathways/community-rights/')}>Pathway</a></dd>
    <dt>Fundraising</dt><dd>Carries over to programme development and partnerships. Add: fluency in financing instruments (grants, technical assistance, loans, guarantees, equity, procurement), how administrators differ from donors, and the sector evidence on what improves outcomes. <a href={url('careers/pathways/finance-partnerships/')}>Pathway</a></dd>
    <dt>Programme management</dt><dd>Carries over to managing mineral-region programmes. Add: the mineral lifecycle, the actors at each stage, and how to separate commitments from implementation and measured outcomes. <a href={url('learn/the-lifecycle/')}>The lifecycle</a></dd>
    <dt>Policy and advocacy</dt><dd>Carries over to resource governance. Add: fiscal regimes, licensing, revenue sharing and transparency standards. <a href={url('careers/pathways/policy-governance/')}>Pathway</a></dd>
    <dt>Monitoring and evaluation</dt><dd>Carries over to evidence roles. Add: the sector&rsquo;s data sources and their limits, and the discipline of keeping allegations, responses, findings and outcomes apart. <a href={url('careers/pathways/research-data/')}>Pathway</a></dd>
    <dt>Livelihoods and enterprise</dt><dd>Carries over to circularity and informal-sector inclusion. Add: extended producer responsibility, battery recycling hazards, and why recycling cannot replace new supply in the near term. <a href={url('careers/pathways/circularity/')}>Pathway</a></dd>
  </dl>
  <h2>How to judge the substance of a job</h2>
  <p>Titles say little. Before you apply, try to answer:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Decision authority.</strong> Which decisions can this role change, and which can it only report on?</li>
    <li><strong>Accountability.</strong> Is the role answerable to the communities or workers it concerns, or only to the employer?</li>
    <li><strong>Independence.</strong> Can the person raise problems without risk to their position? Who reviews their findings?</li>
    <li><strong>Resources.</strong> Is there a budget and a team, or one person covering a whole region?</li>
    <li><strong>Influence on operations.</strong> Does the role sit close enough to operating decisions to change them?</li>
    <li><strong>Travel and safety.</strong> What field conditions, security arrangements and duty of care come with the job?</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>A note on consulting</h2>
  <p>Much of this field runs on short assignments. Consultancy can build breadth quickly, but it can also mean irregular income, no benefits, and work that ends before results are known. Distinguish assignments open to individuals from tenders open only to firms, and read the terms of reference before deciding whether an assignment fits your situation.</p>
